// std::clock — the portable `Clock` pacing boundary.
//
// The corpus's time-facing canaries and the `samples/cli` pacing samples each
// declare a package-local `public boundary trait Clock { machine
// sleep(milliseconds: u32); }` — the Win32 `Sleep` / POSIX `nanosleep` seam —
// and then require a selected provider for their own nominal trait. Provider
// selection is nominal (owning package + trait name), so bundled library code
// can never satisfy a consumer-declared trait. This module is the canonical
// `omega_language_std` `Clock` those consumers can select instead; migrating
// a local `Clock` is a one-line `use omega_language_std::clock;`.
//
// Surface: `sleep(milliseconds)` only — the sole operation every one of those
// declarations shares. Monotonic and wall-clock reads stay on `Time`
// (`time.omg` over the canonical toolchain-settled `TimeHost` seam), so this
// boundary carries no clock-read constants it cannot honor.
//
// The package Build selects one ordinary implementation for each supported
// deployment target:
//
// * `windows_x86_64` binds kernel32 `Sleep`, whose signature is the
//   requirement's, as the leaf itself.
// * `macos_arm64` has no kernel millisecond sleep with the requirement's
//   shape. Its `DarwinArm64Clock::sleep` is an ordinary checked adapter
//   over a std-private `poll` requirement that reaches `Clock`, realized by
//   the libSystem `poll` import (see its `clock_impl.omg`).
// * `linux_x86_64` uses a checked adapter over the integer-only kernel poll
//   wait, preserving the full u32 duration by splitting signed-safe waits.
// * `linux_arm64` constructs a checked seconds/nanoseconds request and lends
//   its storage to kernel nanosleep, rather than passing milliseconds as an
//   address. Both Linux adapters build through native artifact admission;
//   waiting behavior still requires validation on a Linux runner.
//
// `macos_x86_64` and freestanding `uefi_x86_64` retain their missing Clock
// selection. Console support does not imply a Clock implementation. Consumers
// name this module with `use omega_language_std::clock;`.
use targets::linux_x86_64::clock_impl;
use targets::linux_arm64::clock_impl;
use targets::macos_arm64::clock_impl;
use targets::windows_x86_64::clock_impl;

public boundary trait Clock {
    // Block the caller for `milliseconds`.
    machine sleep(milliseconds: u32) invokes DarwinClockWait;
    invokes LinuxArmClockWait;
    invokes LinuxClockWait;
    invokes WindowsClockSleep;
    reaches Clock;
}
